Ozko grlo in učinek ustanovitelja
Dva dogodka zmanjšata raznolikost hitreje kot običajen zdrs:

ozko grlo (bottleneck): velika populacija se za nekaj generacij močno zmanjša, nato se spet poveča,
učinek ustanovitelja (founder effect): novo populacijo ustanovi majhno število osebkov iz večje populacije (npr. kolonizacija otoka).

Za oba potrebujemo lokus z več aleli, da vidimo izgubo redkih alelov. Frekvence vseh alelov v naslednji generaciji so multinomsko porazdeljene (posplošitev binomske porazdelitve na več alelov):

zdrs_alelov &lt;- function(velikosti, frek0, ponovitve = 200) {
  map_dfr(1:ponovitve, \(r) {
    f &lt;- frek0
    map_dfr(seq_along(velikosti), \(t) {
      f &lt;&lt;- as.vector(rmultinom(1, size = 2 * velikosti[t], prob = f)) / (2 * velikosti[t])
      tibble(generacija = t, bogastvo = sum(f &gt; 0), He = 1 - sum(f^2))
    })
  }) |&gt;
    group_by(generacija) |&gt;
    summarise(across(c(bogastvo, He), mean))
}

# 20 alelov: nekaj pogostih in veliko redkih
frek0 &lt;- 0.8^(0:19); frek0 &lt;- frek0 / sum(frek0)
round(frek0, 3)

 [1] 0.202 0.162 0.129 0.104 0.083 0.066 0.053 0.042 0.034 0.027 0.022 0.017
[13] 0.014 0.011 0.009 0.007 0.006 0.005 0.004 0.003

c(bogastvo = sum(frek0 &gt; 0), He = 1 - sum(frek0^2))

 bogastvo        He 
20.000000  0.886297 



grlo &lt;- bind_rows(tibble(generacija = 0, bogastvo = 20, He = 1 - sum(frek0^2)),        # začetno stanje
                  zdrs_alelov(c(rep(2000, 10), rep(10, 5), rep(2000, 30)), frek0))

grlo |&gt;
  mutate(bogastvo = bogastvo / 20, He = He / (1 - sum(frek0^2))) |&gt;
  pivot_longer(-generacija, names_to = "mera", values_to = "delez") |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(generacija, delez, colour = mera)) +
  annotate("rect", xmin = 10.5, xmax = 15.5, ymin = -Inf, ymax = Inf, alpha = 0.15) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = c(bogastvo = "#B5542C", He = "#1F5FA8"),
                      labels = c(bogastvo = "bogastvo alelov", He = "He")) +
  labs(x = "generacija", y = "delež začetne vrednosti", colour = NULL)







Slika 4.7: Ozko grlo: 10 generacij z N = 2000, 5 generacij z N = 10, nato 30 generacij z N = 2000. Obe meri sta prikazani kot delež začetne vrednosti (povprečje 200 ponovitev).





Ozko grlo odstrani predvsem redke alele, zato bogastvo alelov pade hitreje kot He, na katero redki aleli skoraj ne vplivajo. Po ozkem grlu se populacija poveča, raznolikost pa se ne vrne: novi aleli lahko nastanejo samo z mutacijami ali migracijami, kar traja zelo dolgo. To je razlog, da je bogastvo alelov dober pokazatelj nedavnega ozkega grla.

Učinek ustanovitelja
Novo populacijo ustanovi k osebkov (2k kopij gena), naključno izbranih iz velike populacije s frekvencami frek0:

ustanovitelji &lt;- map_dfr(c(1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50), \(k) {
  map_dfr(1:1000, \(r) {
    f &lt;- as.vector(rmultinom(1, size = 2 * k, prob = frek0)) / (2 * k)
    tibble(bogastvo = sum(f &gt; 0) / 20, He = (1 - sum(f^2)) / (1 - sum(frek0^2)))
  }) |&gt;
    summarise(across(everything(), mean)) |&gt;
    mutate(k = k)
})

ustanovitelji |&gt;
  pivot_longer(-k, names_to = "mera", values_to = "delez") |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(k, delez, colour = mera)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) + geom_point(size = 2) +
  scale_x_log10(breaks = c(1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50)) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = c(bogastvo = "#B5542C", He = "#1F5FA8"),
                      labels = c(bogastvo = "bogastvo alelov", He = "He")) +
  labs(x = "število ustanoviteljev (k)", y = "delež izvorne raznolikosti", colour = NULL)







Slika 4.8: Delež bogastva alelov in He izvorne populacije, ki ga ohrani k ustanoviteljev (povprečje 1000 ponovitev).





He se ohrani razmeroma dobro že pri nekaj ustanoviteljih (pričakovano \(1 - \frac{1}{2k}\)), redki aleli pa se izgubijo.






OpombaVprašanje



Islandijo in Grenlandijo so orli kolonizirali iz celine. Katero mero raznolikosti bi pričakovali nižjo na otokih: bogastvo alelov ali He? Kako bi to preverili s SNP-ji, ki imajo vsi le dva alela? (Namig: SFS in redki aleli v vaji 4.)

Bottleneck and founder effect
Two events reduce diversity faster than ordinary drift:

a bottleneck: a large population is strongly reduced for a few generations and then grows again,
a founder effect: a new population is founded by a small number of individuals from a larger population (e.g. colonisation of an island).

For both we need a locus with many alleles to see the loss of rare alleles. The frequencies of all alleles in the next generation are multinomially distributed (the generalisation of the binomial distribution to more than two alleles):

drift_alleles &lt;- function(sizes, freq0, replicates = 200) {
  map_dfr(1:replicates, \(r) {
    f &lt;- freq0
    map_dfr(seq_along(sizes), \(t) {
      f &lt;&lt;- as.vector(rmultinom(1, size = 2 * sizes[t], prob = f)) / (2 * sizes[t])
      tibble(generation = t, richness = sum(f &gt; 0), He = 1 - sum(f^2))
    })
  }) |&gt;
    group_by(generation) |&gt;
    summarise(across(c(richness, He), mean))
}

# 20 alleles: a few common and many rare
freq0 &lt;- 0.8^(0:19); freq0 &lt;- freq0 / sum(freq0)
round(freq0, 3)

 [1] 0.202 0.162 0.129 0.104 0.083 0.066 0.053 0.042 0.034 0.027 0.022 0.017
[13] 0.014 0.011 0.009 0.007 0.006 0.005 0.004 0.003

c(richness = sum(freq0 &gt; 0), He = 1 - sum(freq0^2))

 richness        He 
20.000000  0.886297 



bottleneck &lt;- bind_rows(tibble(generation = 0, richness = 20, He = 1 - sum(freq0^2)),   # starting state
                        drift_alleles(c(rep(2000, 10), rep(10, 5), rep(2000, 30)), freq0))

bottleneck |&gt;
  mutate(richness = richness / 20, He = He / (1 - sum(freq0^2))) |&gt;
  pivot_longer(-generation, names_to = "measure", values_to = "proportion") |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(generation, proportion, colour = measure)) +
  annotate("rect", xmin = 10.5, xmax = 15.5, ymin = -Inf, ymax = Inf, alpha = 0.15) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = c(richness = "#B5542C", He = "#1F5FA8"),
                      labels = c(richness = "allelic richness", He = "He")) +
  labs(x = "generation", y = "proportion of starting value", colour = NULL)







Slika 8.7: Bottleneck: 10 generations with N = 2000, 5 generations with N = 10, then 30 generations with N = 2000. Both measures are shown as a proportion of the starting value (mean of 200 replicates).





A bottleneck mainly removes rare alleles, so allelic richness drops faster than He, which is hardly affected by rare alleles. After the bottleneck the population grows, but diversity does not come back: new alleles can only arise by mutation or migration, which takes a very long time. This is why allelic richness is a good indicator of a recent bottleneck.

Founder effect
A new population is founded by k individuals (2k gene copies) drawn at random from a large population with frequencies freq0:

founders &lt;- map_dfr(c(1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50), \(k) {
  map_dfr(1:1000, \(r) {
    f &lt;- as.vector(rmultinom(1, size = 2 * k, prob = freq0)) / (2 * k)
    tibble(richness = sum(f &gt; 0) / 20, He = (1 - sum(f^2)) / (1 - sum(freq0^2)))
  }) |&gt;
    summarise(across(everything(), mean)) |&gt;
    mutate(k = k)
})

founders |&gt;
  pivot_longer(-k, names_to = "measure", values_to = "proportion") |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(k, proportion, colour = measure)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) + geom_point(size = 2) +
  scale_x_log10(breaks = c(1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50)) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = c(richness = "#B5542C", He = "#1F5FA8"),
                      labels = c(richness = "allelic richness", He = "He")) +
  labs(x = "number of founders (k)", y = "proportion of source diversity", colour = NULL)







Slika 8.8: Proportion of the allelic richness and He of the source population retained by k founders (mean of 1000 replicates).





He is retained relatively well even with a few founders (expected \(1 - \frac{1}{2k}\)), while rare alleles are lost.






OpombaQuestion



Iceland and Greenland were colonised by eagles from the mainland. Which measure of diversity would you expect to be lower on the islands: allelic richness or He? How would you check this with SNPs, which all have only two alleles? (Hint: SFS and rare alleles in Practical 4.)
